built.summary:
I finished all three parts. The kept notice corner is unchanged: its preview drawn before and after is identical in every pixel.

**1. Street scripts (finding F5).** build_street.py now takes settings after "--":
- `--out` and `--name` (where the files go and what they're called);
- `--colliery on|off`;
- `--carriageway` and `--pavement` (the lane's widths);
- `--length`, `--slope` and `--cottages`;
- `--textures` (where the surface pictures are).

With no settings it builds today's pit village street. I added two more things it needed:
- **`--behind`:** how far the lane runs back past its mouth, so a lane can open onto a cross street (W3 needs one).
- **A guard:** it now stops rather than write over existing files unless `--replace` is given. Rebuilding the real pit village street on purpose now needs `--replace`.

I also found and fixed a hidden trap. The colour settings saved in street.blend (exposure 0.6, "Medium High Contrast", 16 samples) were only set when previews were drawn. A rebuild without `--preview` saved a different look, which would have quietly changed the notice corner's pictures. My first "after" picture differed by 7 levels on average for exactly this reason. Every build now saves the same settings.

In set_dressing.py:
- `export_for_film` takes a folder;
- the 4-degree ground fallback is a setting (`set_ground_fallback(slope, level_from_y)`);
- `place_prop` can read from another prop library (`folder=`).

I tested a Works-style lane (6 m road, 2.2 m pavements, 22 m long, level, no colliery) and its overview looks right.

**2. Prop library (finding F11).** The download, convert and library scripts now take a choices list (`--choices FILE`), a library (`--library`) and a scratch folder (`--scratch`); the contact-sheet scripts take `--library` too. The pit village library is untouched. Chapter 2's list is in `chapter_2_choices.py`, and its props go to `assets/models/inflation-chapter-2-newcomers/props/` with their own index.json.

Every name was in the saved Poly Haven lists, and the downloader checks names again live before fetching. Fetched and converted:
- **hand_truck:** 1.40 m tall, matches Poly Haven's size. Its frame is red-painted; if that draws the eye in No. 2's yard, the Works scene can darken it with `set_dressing.tint`.
- **white_sandstone_bricks and large_red_bricks:** saved into textures/ in the street's naming (2k colour, 1k normal; one picture covers 2 x 2 m).

**3. Twelve props built by script** (`build_chapter_2_props.py`, pictures painted by `paint_chapter_2_pictures.py`):
- gate boards "No. 1" and "No. 2";
- the bank's hanging sign and brass plate, both "BANK";
- steel drums "PETROL" and "DIESEL";
- a crate stencilled "THIS WAY UP" on both long sides;
- chain and padlock, lunch tin, jacket and cap on a nail, tin mug;
- a pay envelope "WAGES".

Each is in real metres, stands on the ground, and has one root named after it. Each sign's lettering is on its own named material, listed in index.json, so the film can keep it sharp. The only digits are "1" and "2". I built a new nailed crate rather than reuse wooden-chest, because that model is a lidded chest with iron handles.

Three things the Works scene will need to know:
- **The WAGES envelope can't be read from W4's camera.** At its real size (11 cm) the word is readable only within about 3 m, and the plan puts it 6 m from W4's camera. It will need a typed tag in the film, or a bigger envelope.
- **The drums need turning to face the camera.** Each word wraps about 86 degrees round the drum.
- **The cap may need hiding.** Seen crown-on, a hung cap made an oval over the collar that read like a faceless head. I tilted and darkened it; it is a separate object, "cap", so it can be hidden if a painting turns it into a head.

findings.problem:
The time cut swaps the whole projected picture (plate to helper plate). It does not build the plan's 'state picture': the main painting, with only the men's outlines and their shadow area taken from the other painting. In the free test this looks fine, because the two plates are renders of one scene. With two separate Flux paintings, every brushstroke in the frame would change at the cut.

The area to swap is also far larger than 'the men and their shadows'. I measured how the plate and helper plate differ:
- 20.5% of the frame by more than 8 levels;
- 37% by more than 2 levels;
- 45 to 64% of the rows in the lower half;
- the right-hand house wall is 40% brighter in red without the men, because they block the fire's light.

Picture: /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-Music/02fc5154-76f5-5aac-a505-860164e7e921/scratchpad/redesign/check-round-1/plate-vs-helper-x6.png

In both films the brazier's live smoke drifts in front of the hand-on-bar man. He comes out as a washed-out, see-through grey figure: a ghost in the middle of the group (stills-painted/still-1.00.jpg and stills-silhouettes/still-1.00.jpg). The other points the helper raised are still open:
- the stooped man's hands rest on the hot rim;
- the women in head shawls read as nuns;
- from behind, the walkers don't look as if they are walking.
